WEBVTT

1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,805
Many organizations federate cloud access through an identity provider.

2
00:00:04,805 --> 00:00:13,950
Instead of managing separate cloud passwords for every user, employees sign in through single sign-on using corporate identity controls.

3
00:00:13,950 --> 00:00:22,321
Groups, conditional access, MFA, device posture, and role assignment can then determine which cloud roles a person may assume.

4
00:00:22,321 --> 00:00:32,008
This centralizes lifecycle management: when someone changes roles or leaves the organization, access can be adjusted from the identity provider.

5
00:00:32,008 --> 00:00:37,511
Multi-factor authentication is especially important for sensitive cloud access.

6
00:00:37,511 --> 00:00:42,626
A password alone should not be enough to reach powerful administrative roles.

7
00:00:42,626 --> 00:00:51,384
MFA does not prove that every action is safe, but it reduces the chance that a stolen password becomes immediate control-plane access.

8
00:00:51,384 --> 00:01:01,536
Conditional access policies can add more context, such as trusted devices, location, risk signals, or stronger authentication for higher-risk actions.

9
00:01:01,536 --> 00:01:04,869
Authentication and authorization are different.

10
00:01:04,869 --> 00:01:08,279
Authentication answers who the user or workload is.

11
00:01:08,279 --> 00:01:11,612
Authorization answers what that principal can do.

12
00:01:11,612 --> 00:01:21,067
A user may authenticate successfully through SSO and MFA but still be unauthorized to delete a database, modify a firewall rule, or read a secret.

13
00:01:21,067 --> 00:01:27,500
Cloud IAM security depends on both: strong sign-in controls and carefully designed permissions.

14
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:30,600
Temporary access reduces standing privilege.

15
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:43,620
Role assumption, just-in-time access, privileged access workflows, and short-lived credentials can let authorized users perform tasks without keeping powerful permissions active all the time.

16
00:01:43,620 --> 00:01:52,455
Long-lived access keys and standing administrator grants create operational risk because they can be copied, forgotten, or misused.

17
00:01:52,455 --> 00:01:59,585
Where long-lived credentials still exist, they should be owned, rotated, monitored, and reduced over time.

18
00:01:59,585 --> 00:02:03,693
Just-in-time access is especially useful for powerful roles.

19
00:02:03,693 --> 00:02:11,908
Instead of leaving administrator access active all day, an engineer can request time-limited elevation for a specific task.

20
00:02:11,908 --> 00:02:16,481
The approval, duration, reason, and resulting actions can be logged.

21
00:02:16,481 --> 00:02:25,316
This creates a better record for audit and incident response while reducing the window in which a compromised session can cause damage.

22
00:02:25,316 --> 00:02:29,656
Long-lived keys should be treated as exceptions, not the default.

23
00:02:29,656 --> 00:02:37,019
If a key is needed, it should have an owner, purpose, scope, rotation plan, storage expectation, and monitoring.

24
00:02:37,019 --> 00:02:42,754
Many environments still contain old keys created for scripts, vendors, or migrations.

25
00:02:42,754 --> 00:02:48,489
Finding and reducing those keys is often one of the highest-value IAM cleanup projects.

26
00:02:48,489 --> 00:02:52,674
Federation also improves offboarding and emergency response.

27
00:02:52,674 --> 00:03:02,052
If a user leaves the company or a credential is suspected of compromise, central identity controls can remove or restrict cloud access quickly.

28
00:03:02,052 --> 00:03:07,477
Local-only cloud users and unmanaged keys are harder to find and harder to disable.

29
00:03:07,477 --> 00:03:13,290
That is why mature programs reduce standalone accounts and monitor exceptions closely.
